{"step_index":5653,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:45:00Z","thinking":"Analyzing each shortage item. The normalized name is checked against a warehouse item dictionary. Based on item name comparison, the goal is to classify if the shortage item corresponds to an existing warehouse item.\n\n\nDirect matches are flagged and recorded as \"exact\" with high confidence. Items requiring review are noted, indicating a potential name and formulation match but requiring pharmacist verification. Failing an exact match, a search of similar items is performed; if results are found, those pairs are classified.\n\n\nWhen a direct match isn't found, the code checks for ambiguous matches. If ambiguous matches are present, the top candidates are prepared for an LLM to resolve by generating structured data to facilitate the LLM's decision-making. If there is a review candidate, it's added to a list for pharmacist review, along with a reason noting potential formulation differences requiring expert evaluation.\n\n\nChecking item strength consistency; numerical values and slash formats are compared across shortage and warehouse items to identify mismatches, setting a \"no_match\" status if differences exist. I'm also examining the presence and consistency of combination suffixes (e.g., \"co\", \"plus\") between the two item names. Any detected discrepancy in either strength or suffix triggers a \"no_match\" classification.\n\n\nExamining commercial prefixes (\"new\", \"نيو\") to identify discrepancies; a mismatch results in a \"no_match\" status. Brand similarity is assessed by extracting and comparing brand tokens, calculating fuzzy string matching ratios, and considering the first word's similarity. If the first words of the brands match or have a high fuzzy ratio, I boost the similarity score.\n\n\nBrand prefix integrity is checked by comparing whether both item names start with \"ال\". If one has it and the other doesn't, a fuz